WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
I realized recently that I didn't really understand how a prism works, and I suspect

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:11.460
most people out there don't either.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.780
Arguably this is one of the most widely recognized physics experiments ever.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.660
After all, how many others have earned a place as an iconic album cover?

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.260
Sure, some of Pink Floyd's design choices run completely contrary to the actual physics.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.680
Like why did they make the light inside the prism white?

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.140
And bafflingly, why would you draw all the colors as a discrete set, like a child making

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.180
a rainbow, despite the fact that one of the key points in Newton's original experiment

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.840
involving prisms was that sunlight contains a continuous spectrum of colors?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.720
Still, setting that aside, it's cool that it's in pop culture at all, and any self-respecting

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.920
physics enthusiast should know how it works, but the thing I realized is that my understanding

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.280
hit a wall pretty quickly if pressed.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.140
You see, the standard explanation, what you might hear in a high school physics class

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.340
for example, goes something like this.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:05.140
When light enters a medium, like glass, it slows down, in the sense that if you look

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:10.740
at the crests of the wave, in a vacuum those crests are traveling at c, the speed of light,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.740
but inside the glass those crests will be traveling a little bit slower.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.500
And the specific ratio between the speed of light in a vacuum and the speed inside a medium

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.180
like this is called the index of refraction for that medium.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.940
The reason we use the word refraction instead of the index of slowing is that if a beam

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:34.140
of light enters this glass at an angle, then a consequence of this slowdown is that it

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.820
bends a little bit, or using the lingo, it refracts.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.860
The way my high school physics teacher always explained this was to imagine a tank going

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.440
from some region where it can travel relatively quickly, like concrete, into something slower,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.980
like mud, where if it's coming in at an angle, then as one of its treads hits the slow region

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.140
first, that tread will be going slower while the other one is faster, causing the whole

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.260
tank to steer a little bit until that second tread also enters the mud, then it continues

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.340
straight just traveling a little slower.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.420
We'll get back to the actual reason for bending in a bit, but at this point the high

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:13.100
school physics students typically learn a law known as Snell's law which specifies

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.420
exactly how much things bend.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.580
If you draw a line perpendicular to the boundary between the glass and water, and consider

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.240
the angle between that perpendicular line and the beam of light, Snell's law tells

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.860
us that the sine of this angle divided by the speed of the light is always a constant.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:34.440
So the slower the light, the lower that angle will be, and that lets you calculate how much

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.260
things refract.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.160
What's going on with a prism, then, is that the specific amount that light slows down

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.940
depends a little bit on its frequency.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:48.520
For example, blue light, which has a relatively high frequency, would get slowed down more

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.360
aggressively than red light, which has a relatively low frequency.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.600
Most of the light you see is not a clean pure sine wave, in particular the white light coming

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.600
from the sun is not a clean sine wave, it's something much messier, but it can be expressed

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:08.320
as a sum of a bunch of clean sine waves, each one corresponding to a pure spectral color.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.000
So when you shine white light into a prism like this, all those different components

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.840
get refracted by slightly different amounts, causing this iconic separation of the pure

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.600
rainbow colors.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.400
So that is the standard explanation, and it's not wrong per se, it's just that all of

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.000
the key components are handed down from on high.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.140
Why would light slow down like this?

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.720
And what exactly do we mean by slowing down?

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.680
And even if you understand that, why would the amount that it slows down have anything

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.200
to do with the color of the light?

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.840
Is that just a coincidence or is it necessary?

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.720
If you have a sufficiently high standard for explanations, you want both of these facts

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:47.960
to feel discovered, rather than feeling like they were handed down.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.880
The first explanation I saw that started to give this feeling came from the Feynman lectures

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.800
on the matter, and a lot of what I'd like to do with this video is simply animate a

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.220
lot of the key points that he makes there.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.040
It involves really digging in to think about each individual wiggling charge in the material

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.180
and the propagating light waves caused by each one of those charges and how all of them

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.800
superimpose on top of each other, which feels like it should be a complete mess, but it

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.920
actually works out to be not only understandable, but satisfyingly explanatory.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:21.240
For example, it explains why it has to depend on color, and the key intuition there really

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.520
comes down to what happens if you're bad at pushing a child on a swing.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.080
Bear with me, I promise that'll make sense later.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.640
Also, when I mentioned on Patreon the intention to cover this topic, a lot of people had a

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.360
lot of questions about the index of refraction.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.180
For example, numerous people asked about how it's possible for this number to be lower

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.620
than 1, which really does happen, despite that seeming to imply the impossibility of

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.380
something traveling faster than the speed of light.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.500
There was also a question about birefringence, which is where a material can have two different

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.360
indices of refraction causing you to see double when you look through it.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.740
And that actually ties in really nicely to putting in the final puzzle piece from the

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.560
last two videos about the barber pole phenomenon.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.300
And a couple people also asked about why light slowing down would imply a bending like this.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.720
And I agree, that deserves a better explanation than the tank analogy.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.760
I promise we'll get to all of these questions later, but it makes sense to first lay down

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.900
some groundwork by spending the bulk of our time on the key question of why passing through

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.560
a medium would change the speed of a light wave at all.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.900
And for this, I want you to think of your material, like glass, as being broken up into

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:34.140
a bunch of distinct layers, all perpendicular to the direction the light is traveling.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.940
And we'll start by focusing our attention on the effect of just one of those layers

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.380
on the light wave.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.220
The true effect would be miniscule, but if you'll let me exaggerate it for a moment,

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:47.500
what it does is kick back the phase of the wave.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.580
And maybe it's worth a brief aside to make sure we're all on the same page when it comes

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.080
to wave terminology.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.360
If you go and graph the function sine of x, when you put some term in front of it, affecting

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.360
how high that wave oscillates up and down, that's what we call the amplitude, when you

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.980
put a term in front of x, this will affect how rapidly it oscillates.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.940
If this is meant to describe a wave over time, that term would be called the angular frequency,

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:13.740
whereas if it's meant to describe a wave over space, that constant would be called

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.020
the wave number.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.020
Then if you were to add some other constant inside that sine function, and notice how

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:23.780
as you change what that constant is, it sort of slides the wave left and right, that term

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:26.620
describes the phase of the wave.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.980
So when I say that our light wave hitting a layer of glass causes its phase to get kicked

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.940
back, I mean if you take whatever function describes it before it hits the glass, then

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.140
the function describing it after that looks almost the same, just with a little extra

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.340
something added to the input of that sine function.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.200
Like I said, in reality that'll be a very small number, something proportional to the

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:50.260
infinitesimal thickness of that layer, but I'll keep drawing it as something exaggerated

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:54.860
and keep track of the value of that phase kick over here on the left.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.660
Let's say you go and add a bunch of other layers of the glass, each one also applying

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.360
their own kickback to the phase of the wave.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.500
The question for you is what does that new wave look like?

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.500
If the value of that phase kick applied by each layer is something really close to zero,

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.720
then the wave is hardly affected at all.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.860
But the larger that phase kick, the more the wave kind of gets squished together among

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.820
all those layers.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:21.340
Admittedly right here it looks all kaleidoscopic and weird, but that's really just because

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:26.020
I have a discrete set of layers, each applying an unrealistically large kick.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:30.500
Notice what happens if I smooth it out by doubling the density of layers, but having

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.300
each one only apply half the phase kick.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:37.480
And then I do that again, I double the density of the layers, but have each one only apply

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.740
half the phase kick.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:44.380
As I continue this over and over, approaching a situation where you have a continuum of

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:50.840
glass, each layer applying just a tiny infinitesimal phase kick, what you end up with is identical

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:56.320
to, indistinguishable from, a wave that's simply traveling slower, oscillating up and

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.080
down with the same frequency, but with a wavelength that's been kind of scrunched up.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.800
This right here is the first key idea with the index of refraction.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.520
Instead of asking why does light slow down in glass, what we really need to ask is why

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.840
does its interaction with a single layer of that glass cause a kickback to the phase of

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.200
the wave.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.100
And then when we want to get quantitative and understand exactly how much the light

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:24.560
slows down, which is critical for understanding why it depends on color, instead the real

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.240
question is how strong is that phase kick?

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.300
From here it's helpful to turn back to the fundamentals of what light even is.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.480
This is something we talked a lot about in the last video, but a little review never

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.060
hurts so let me go over the essentials.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:43.120
As many of you know, light is a wave in the electromagnetic field, but here we'll just

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.340
be drawing the electric field.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:50.160
The electric field associates each point in 3D space with a little 3-dimensional vector

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.560
telling you what force would be applied to a hypothetical unit charge sitting at that

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.160
point in space.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:01.940
The key thing going on with light is that if you have a charged particle and something

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:06.820
causes it to wiggle up and down, that results in these propagating ripples in the electric

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:12.920
field away from the charge, and that propagation is traveling at the speed c, the speed of

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:13.920
light.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.520
Whenever those ripples happen to reach another charged particle, they cause it to wiggle

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.280
up and down, albeit a little more weakly than the initial wiggle, and that in turn causes

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:24.500
its own propagations.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:28.400
The way we described this in the last video was that if at some point in time a charge

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:33.940
is accelerating, then after a little delay, which depends on this speed c, the existence

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.640
of that acceleration induces a force on another charge.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:42.400
We went over the specific force law describing this, it's something that can be derived

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.220
downstream of Maxwell's equations, but for our purposes here, the main thing to tuck

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:50.800
away in your mind is that the amount of time it takes that initial acceleration to cause

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.340
any kind of influence elsewhere travels at exactly the speed c.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:59.580
And really, you should think of c not so much as the speed of light per se, but as the speed

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.120
of causality.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.520
It determines how fast any kind of influence travels, it's just that one of multiple

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.600
consequences of that is that it's the speed of light.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:13.780
In particular, when you get a charge oscillating up and down in a nice clean sinusoidal motion,

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:17.820
you can think of these rippling effects in the electric field as describing the force

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:24.360
that would be applied to another charge sitting there as a result of that past acceleration.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:28.040
I will freely admit that I had a bit too much fun in that video just simulating how the

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:32.380
electric field responds to accelerating charges, and that I'm kind of doing the same thing

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:36.900
here, but there are two important facts for our pursuit of the index of refraction.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:41.720
The first is that when you have multiple different charges oscillating up and down, the net effect

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:46.780
on the electric field is just the sum of what it would be for each individual charge, which

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.760
is kind of what you would expect.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:52.760
The way that it shakes out is that if you have a row of charges oscillating in sync

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:58.200
with each other, or for our purposes today, a plane of charges, all wiggling up and down

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:03.700
in sync within that plane, then the effects of each individual charge tend to cancel each

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:09.240
other out in most directions, except perpendicular to that plane, they actually constructively

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.240
interfere.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:13.000
This is how you can get a concentrated beam of light.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.140
That's the important thing.

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:18.560
If you have a layer of charges wiggling up and down in sync with each other, then even

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:23.340
far away from that layer, it produces this nice sinusoidal wave in the electric field

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:27.840
that we're so fond of drawing to represent light.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:31.380
When I draw a light wave like this, it's really only depicting the electric field on

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.380
a single one-dimensional line.

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:38.180
A more full picture of light in three dimensions would look something more like this.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:42.860
That tends to be a little bit busier, so usually we just draw the sine wave.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:48.180
So, thinking back to the question of why interactions with a layer of material would

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.180
cause a kickback to the phase of the wave, let's start thinking it through.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:57.400
When a light beam enters a material, like glass, then it causes all of the charges inside

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:02.440
that material, you know, electrons, or maybe the occasional ion, to wiggle up and down

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:04.520
in response to that light wave.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.520
You might think that adding together all the propagations from all those charges is a complete

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.520
nightmare, but we can think about it one layer at a time.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.600
As the light wave causes this layer to wiggle up and down, that wiggling produces its own

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:23.480
second-order light wave at the same frequency, and it propagates in both directions perpendicular

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:24.760
to that layer.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:29.720
The overall electric field, then, looks like the initial incoming light wave added together

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:32.600
with the second-order wave.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:36.920
By far the most distracting part of what's going on here is everything on the left, and

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:40.160
this actually corresponds to the light being reflected back.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:43.980
And from experience, you all know that when you look at water or you look at glass, light

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:47.260
not only goes through it, but some of it gets reflected back.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:51.820
And we could have a whole interesting discussion on quantifying exactly how much, but in the

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:56.380
spirit of staying focused, we will completely ignore that for today, and only focus on what's

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.420
happening to the right of that layer.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.860
You can probably predict what I'm going to say.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:05.380
It turns out that when you add that second-order oscillation, the overall effect is almost

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:10.240
identical to the incoming light, but just shifted back in phase by a little bit.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:14.720
And then because many successive shifts to the phase like this are the same thing as

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:19.480
light slowing down, this will ultimately explain the index of refraction.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:23.540
But of course, the sufficiently curious viewers will now be raising their hands and asking,

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:26.240
why is that the effect when you add them together?

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.040
And so here it might be worth a little sidebar on how to think about adding two waves together.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.520
If you draw some sine wave with some particular amplitude, some specific frequency, and some

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.740
specific phase, and then you draw another sine wave, also with its own amplitude, frequency,

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:45.040
and phase, in general, it's very hard to think about what the sum of those two waves

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:52.560
should look like as you tweak those initial parameters.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:57.020
In the specific case where the frequencies are the same, which is true for our example,

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:01.400
the result will also look like a sine wave with that same frequency.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:05.340
But even then, it's a little tricky to think about exactly how to describe that wave.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:10.500
It has some amplitude and some phase, and if I ask you to concretely compute both of

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:15.500
those numbers, based on the amplitudes and phases of the initial waves, it's not immediately

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:19.180
clear how you would do that without maybe throwing a bunch of trig identities at the

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.260
problem.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:22.560
But here's a really nice way to think about it.

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:28.560
Imagine that first wave describes the y-component of some rotating vector.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:33.240
The length of that vector corresponds with the amplitude of our wave, and then the initial

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:37.680
rotation of that vector corresponds with the phase of our wave.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.400
And then similarly think of that second wave as describing the y-component of another rotating

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:47.280
vector, where again the amplitude corresponds with the length of that vector, and the phase

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:52.840
of the wave tells us the initial angle of that vector.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:56.960
Now to think about the sum of the two waves, just think about adding those two vectors

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.400
tip to tail.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:02.760
And because they both have the same frequency as both of them rotate, their sum kind of

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:07.400
rotates in lockstep with them.

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:11.360
So if you want to think about the amplitude of our resulting wave, it comes down to the

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:16.320
length of this vector sum, and similarly the phase corresponds to the angle of that vector

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:17.360
that we saw in the problem.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:21.360
In some cases this tells you things that you probably already knew, like if the two phases

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.800
happen to be the same, then you get constructive interference and you have a bigger wave that

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:26.800
results.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:31.680
And if the phases were 180 degrees out of sync, then you get deconstructive interference

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.440
with a relatively small resulting wave.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:38.680
What's a little bit less obvious, but what's crucial for our discussion here, is that if

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:43.720
the phase of that second wave happens to be exactly 90 degrees behind the phase of the

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:48.080
first, so kind of a quarter cycle out of sync, and if that second wave is also very

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:53.040
small compared to the first, then if you look at the little vector sum on the lower left,

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:58.160
you'll notice how this means that the resulting wave is almost identical to the initial wave,

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:01.400
but just shifted back in its phase by a tiny bit.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:08.560
Moreover, the size of that phase shift depends on the specific amplitude of that second wave.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.040
So looking back at our previous animation, where we have some wiggling charges in a layer

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:17.840
of glass causing these second order propagations that need to be added together with the incoming

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:23.320
light, the way it works out is that the phase of that second wave is exactly a quarter of

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:26.200
a cycle behind the phase of the first.

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:29.300
So when you add them together, you get this little phase shift.

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:34.400
And then, critically, the size of that phase shift is bigger when that second order wave

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:38.640
is larger, and then smaller when that second order wave is smaller.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:43.200
Again, the very curious viewers will be raising their hands and saying, why does it work out

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.160
to be exactly a quarter of a cycle behind?

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:50.120
There is a very nice reason, but it's just a little too much detail for us today.

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:53.280
If you're curious, I highly encourage you to take a look at the Feynman lectures on

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:54.600
the matter.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:59.240
For our purposes, step back for a second and think about what you need to explain the key

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:05.000
question of prisms, which is why the index of refraction would depend on color at all.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:09.400
