00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:14.820
of the wave, and that phase kick depends on the strength of the second order wave resulting

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:18.100
from charge oscillations in a layer of that glass.

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:23.620
So you need to drill in and understand exactly how much those charges wiggle in response

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:25.620
to an incoming light wave.

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:30.000
So let's zoom in on that layer and think of each one of those charged particles, and

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.840
even though the specific molecular structure is going to be something very complicated,

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:38.760
we're going to model each one of those charges as if it was bound to some equilibrium position

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.600
by a spring, or maybe a set of springs.

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:45.800
I don't mean this literally, of course, I just mean if we describe the displacement

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:50.640
of this charge from its equilibrium with a little vector x that's going to depend on

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:56.820
time, then in our model, the force applied to the charge, pulling it back to that equilibrium,

00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:01.840
is going to be something proportional to the size of that displacement, with a little proportionality

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.240
constant k.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.900
This is the same law that governs how springs work.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:10.120
You might ask if that's accurate, and the idea is that for very small displacements,

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:12.240
it's actually a really good approximation.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.960
This is a very common thing to do throughout physics, we would call it a linear restoring

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:16.960
force.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:20.960
The idea is that maybe the actual force law depends on the position in a much more complicated

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:25.800
way, but we're basically taking a low order approximation near the equilibrium.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:30.280
If I just run this as a simulation, plugging in this force law, here's what that displacement

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.580
looks like as a function of time.

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:36.620
What you get looks like a sine wave, this is called simple harmonic motion, and the

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:40.540
frequency of this wave is going to matter a lot for you and me, and finding that comes

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:45.080
down to solving a certain differential equation, because the force is really the same thing

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:50.800
as mass times acceleration, and the acceleration is the same thing as the second derivative

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:52.340
of that displacement.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.580
So what we're saying is we want some function whose second derivative looks like a certain

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.440
constant times that function itself.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:02.840
Many differential equations students among you might enjoy thinking about how you solve

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:03.840
this.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:07.440
I won't go over the full details, but the answer is reasonably intuitive, and anyone

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:10.400
who knows a little calculus can just check it for themselves.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:14.240
The way it shakes out is that if the initial condition is that our little charge has a

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:19.400
velocity of zero, but it's offset from the equilibrium by a little vector x naught, then

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:25.380
the way it evolves over time looks like x naught multiplied by a cosine expression.

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:29.360
So the amplitude of this wave is kind of uninteresting, it just depends on how far we pull

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:34.100
things back originally, but the meet is this frequency term, square root of k divided by

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.400
m.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:39.120
And if you think about it, this should hopefully be at least a little intuitive.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:44.320
For example, if you increase k, which is kind of like increasing the strength of that spring,

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:47.080
then it results in a faster oscillation.

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:51.560
Whereas if you increase m, the mass of the particle, there's a lot more inertia, and

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:54.220
it results in a slower oscillation.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:58.100
This term, square root of k divided by m, has a special name, it's called the resonant

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:01.160
frequency for our simple harmonic oscillator.

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:05.220
And being a little more precise, I should call this the resonant angular frequency.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:08.460
This is always a little bit of an awkwardness with physics, where whenever you have some

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:12.900
kind of cyclic process, when you give an intuitive description, it's natural to phrase things

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:17.740
in terms of the frequency, the number of cycles that this process makes per unit time.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:21.500
But when doing math, it's often more natural to talk about the angular frequency, which

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:25.780
you could think of as describing how much angle this process covers in radians per unit

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:26.780
time.

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:29.300
So the term is the same as the frequency but multiplied by 2 pi.

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:32.900
So for example, if you have something like a cosine expression, which you might think

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:37.440
of as describing the x-component of a cycling vector like this, then the term sitting right

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:41.500
in front of the t in that cosine is the angular frequency.

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:44.160
This is why angular frequency makes the math a little cleaner.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:47.740
For example, in our simple harmonic motion, the term sitting in front of t looks like

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:52.300
the square root of k divided by m, which I'm writing as omega sub r.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:56.420
Let's package all of that up and call that our solution in the simple case, where there's

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.980
no external force acting on our charged particle.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:04.100
But of course, what we're interested in is what happens when you shine a beam of light

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:08.980
on this material, which intuitively causes this charge to jiggle, but the question is

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:10.540
how much.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:14.460
In our equation, this looks like adding a new force term corresponding to the light

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:15.460
wave.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:20.300
That force oscillates up and down also according to some kind of cosine function, but this

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:25.180
time with a distinct angular frequency that I'm going to call omega sub l.

00:21:25.180 --> 00:21:30.140
E naught here describes the strength of the wave, and q describes the charge of whatever

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:32.000
particle we're modeling.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.340
As usual, it's a lot easier to think about when we only draw a subset of that light wave,

00:21:36.340 --> 00:21:40.380
and in this case we're going to draw it on the plane of the layer of material we care

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:41.380
about.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:45.620
You might think of gusts of wind blowing our little ball on the spring up and down in a

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:47.840
clean sinusoidal pattern.

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:51.520
Or as another analogy, it's similar to pushing a child on a swing.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:56.280
The swing would oscillate on its own due to the force of gravity, but you as the pusher

00:21:56.280 --> 00:22:01.140
are applying an external force which itself is oscillating over time.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:05.880
Although a key difference here is that the frequency of that external force in general

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:10.960
has nothing to do with the resonant frequency of that little oscillator.

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:15.200
The better analogy would be if you're pushing the child on the swing with a cyclic force

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:19.340
that has nothing to do with what the swing naturally wants to do.

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:23.340
And my favorite part in literally trying to do this with my niece is that at some point

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:27.300
she gently murmurs to herself, this isn't how mom does it.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:32.380
Now, in trying to understand how much our charge is oscillating in response to the incoming

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:37.060
light, let me start by just simulating it and plotting the result.

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:40.880
You'll notice that there's a little start-up period where it kind of has to get going,

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:46.100
but then after that, mercifully, it looks nice and clean, just like another sine wave.

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:50.700
Now you might be thinking, yeah yeah, everything is sine waves, but it's important to understand

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:54.740
that this one has a very different character from the sine wave we saw earlier.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:23:00.420
Earlier, without any external forces, the frequency of that wave came down to the spring

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:05.820
constant and the mass, which is to say, it depends exclusively on material properties

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.140
of the glass.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:12.380
By contrast, with this external cycling driving force, the frequency in that steady state

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:15.380
is the same as the frequency of the light.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:19.260
And then in our first case, the amplitude of the wave was kind of uninteresting, it

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:22.660
just depends on how far you pulled the spring out to begin with.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:26.740
But in the second case, the amplitude of this wave is actually where all the interesting

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:28.480
stuff happens.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:32.780
Exactly how much will this charge be oscillating in response to the light wave?

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:37.980
Again, I won't go over the full details of solving this, but any eager calculus students

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:42.980
among you might enjoy going through the exercise where if you just guess that a solution looks

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:47.820
like a cosine wave with the same frequency as the light, and you solve for the amplitude,

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:52.620
you can get a concrete solution to this equation that looks like this.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:57.100
This is worth unpacking for a bit, and just to be clear, this is only describing things

00:23:57.100 --> 00:24:00.780
in the steady state, after things have gotten up and going.

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:04.500
A fully descriptive solution would be notably more complicated.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:08.940
As I said, everything interesting here comes down to the amplitude, which here looks like

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:12.900
a large collection of constants, most of which should be pretty intuitive if you take

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:14.280
a moment to think about it.

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:18.520
